  "Number": 8,
  "Question": "What is the sum of all integer solutions to the following inequality? \\[1 < (x-2)^2 < 25\\]",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\( 10 \\)",
    "B": "\\( 12 \\)",
    "C": "\\( 15 \\)",
    "D": "\\( 19 \\)",
    "E": "\\( 25 \\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "Because \\(x\\) is an integer, \\(x-2\\) is also an integer. Its square is strictly between \\(1\\) and \\(25\\) exactly when\n\\[x-2=\\pm2,\\ \\pm3,\\ \\pm4.\\]\nThese possibilities are exhaustive: magnitudes \\(0\\) and \\(1\\) give squares at most \\(1\\), while magnitudes at least \\(5\\) give squares at least \\(25\\).\n\nAdding \\(2\\) gives the six distinct solutions\n\\[-2,\\ -1,\\ 0,\\ 4,\\ 5,\\ 6.\\]\nPairing values equally far from \\(2\\), their sum is\n\\[(-2+6)+(-1+5)+(0+4)=12.\\]\nThe answer is \\(\\boxed{12}\\), choice B.",
